  "question": {
    "text": "Consider the real valued function $\\mathrm{h}:\\{0,1,2 \\ldots \\ldots 100\\} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{R}$ such that $\\mathrm{h}(0)=5, \\mathrm{~h}(100)=20$ and satisfying $h(p)=\\frac{1}{2}\\{h(p+1)+h(p-1)\\}$ for every $p=1,2 \\ldots .99 .$ Then the value of $h(1)$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "PQikK",
      "text": "$5.15$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "dWGlI",
      "text": "$5.5$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "pWDAG",
      "text": "6",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "vow1j",
      "text": "$6.15$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$h(p)=\\frac{1}{2}(h(p+1)+h(p-1)) \\Rightarrow h(p-1), h(p), h(p+1)$ are in A.P.\r\n$h(100)=h(0)+99 d$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\frac{20-5}{99}=d \\Rightarrow d=\\frac{15}{99} \\Rightarrow h(1)=h(0)+d=5+\\frac{15}{99}=5.15$",
    "image": null
